 a denylist or prefix match (`startswith`, `mcp__server__`), or a refresh or reconnect path skips it.\n  B01.3 The dispatcher rejects a call to any tool name not on the allowlist, instead of looking it up dynamically. | 1) Find the dispatcher (search `tool_use`, `call_tool`, `dispatch`, `execute`, `getattr`, `registry[`). 2) Check it rejects names not on the allowlist with an error. 3) Answer no if it looks tools up with `getattr`, `globals()`, `eval`, or a registry holding more than the allowlist, falls back to calling the MCP server directly, or catches the unknown-tool error and continues.\n  B01.4 Allow and approval decisions never read server-supplied hints or descriptions such as `readOnlyHint` or 'read-only'. | 1) Search for `readOnlyHint`, `destructiveHint`, `annotations`, `description`, and approval or auto-approve code. 2) Check whether any allow, skip-approval, or permission branch reads these. 3) Answer no if any allow or approval decision depends on a server hint or tool description text, including a default that auto-approves tools marked read-only.\n  B01.5 File access is limited to named directories, not the whole filesystem. | 1) Find file tools and mounts (`allowed_directories`, `cwd`, `open(`, `fs.`, `os.ReadFile`, container `volumes`, `mountPath`). 2) Check paths are resolved (`realpath`, `resolve`, `filepath.Clean`) then compared to named directories. 3) Answer no if access is unrestricted, `/` or home is mounted, the check is a string `startsWith`/prefix test without resolving `..` and symlinks, or a shell tool bypasses it.\nB02 Least-privilege access\n  B02.1 The agent's IAM policy or role names specific actions and resources, with no `*` action or resource and no owner, editor, or cluster-admin role. | 1) Find IAM and RBAC files (`*.tf`, `iam*.json`, `Role`, `ClusterRoleBinding`, `RoleBinding`, CloudFormation). 2) Find the policy attached to the agent's identity. 3) Answer no if any `Action` or `Resource` is `*` or a service wildcard (`s3:*`), or it binds `roles/owner`, `roles/editor`, `cluster-admin`, or AdministratorAccess. Answer unknown if the agent's IAM is not in this repo.\n  B02.2 Tenant scope is enforced in the credential or the data layer (token claim, row-level security, or per-tenant account), not by the prompt or by code the model's output controls. | 1) Find how queries and API calls pick a tenant (search `tenant`, `org_id`, row-level security, `CREATE POLICY`, per-tenant credentials). 2) Trace where the tenant value comes from. 3) Answer no if one shared login or key reaches all tenants and the tenant ID comes from the prompt, a tool argument the model fills, or app code filtering after the query.\n  B02.3 Tokens are issued with a set audience, and receiving services reject tokens whose audience does not match. | 1) Find token requests (`audience`, `aud`, `resource`, `scope`, token client setup). 2) Find token validation in receiving services (`verify`, `jwt.decode`, middleware). 3) Answer no if tokens are requested without an audience, validation skips `aud` (`verify_aud=False`, no audience option), or accepts an unrelated audience or wildcard. Answer unknown if receiving services are outside this repo.\n  B02.4 The acting agent is verifiably identified separately from any explicitly delegated user/system authority; it does not copy a login session or inherit a person's unrestricted permissions. | 1) Trace each API credential to its issuer, acting identity, original subject, grants and destination. 2) Accept explicitly authorized user-delegation flows with a separately verified agent and bounded permissions. 3) Answer no for copied login sessions, personal/admin keys or inherited broad user access; a delegated OAuth access token is not by itself a failure. Answer unknown when external issuer/enforcement evidence is unavailable.\nB03 Credential lifetime and handling\n  B03.1 Credentials are fetched at run time from a managed identity or secrets service, not fixed API keys, where short-lived or federated credentials are available. | 1) Find credential setup (`AssumeRole`, IRSA, Workload Identity, Managed Identity, Vault, `os.environ`, `process.env`). 2) Check each credential is fetched at run time. 3) Answer no if a fixed key (`AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `API_KEY` in a Secret or env) is used for a service that offers federated or short-lived credentials, or code falls back to a static key when the managed source fails.\n  B03.2 Credential lifetime and rotation are set in config to bounded values. | 1) Search for lifetime and rotation settings (`DurationSeconds`, `ttl`, `expires_in`, `max_ttl`, rotation job or policy). 2) Check each credential the agent uses has a bounded value. 3) Answer no if any credential has no expiry, no rotation, or an unbounded or very long value. Answer unknown if lifetimes are set in an identity provider outside this repo.\n  B03.3 No long-lived secrets appear in prompts, Dockerfiles or image layers, or checked-in config. | 1) Search prompt templates, Dockerfiles (`ENV`, `ARG`, `COPY .env`), `.env*`, `*.yaml`, `*.tfvars`, `.mcp.json` `env` blocks for key-like literals (`sk-`, `AKIA`, `token:`, `password:`). 2) Check prompt-building code for env values inserted into messages. 3) Answer no if any real secret is checked in, baked into an image, or placed in a prompt; `.gitignore` listing a file does not count if it is committed.\n  B03.4 Where the service supports it, tokens are bound to their holder with mTLS, DPoP, or workload proof tokens. | 1) Find token and client setup for each service (`mTLS`, `client_cert`, `DPoP`, `cert-bound`, SPIFFE). 2) Check whether the service supports holder binding. 3) Answer no if a supporting service uses plain bearer tokens. Answer unknown if support depends on a provider outside this repo and nothing states it.\nB04 Independent revocation\n  B04.1 A revocation control disables the agent's own identity or credentials without disabling the user who launched it. | 1) Find a disable or revoke path (script, runbook, IdP config, `disable`, `revoke`, deny list). 2) Check it targets the agent's own identity or credential. 3) Answer no if the agent uses the user's token, so revocation disables the user too, or no revoke path exists. Answer unknown if revocation lives only in an identity provider outside this repo.\n  B04.2 Revocation is applied at a trusted enforcement point (identity provider, gateway, or target service) and does not need the agent's cooperation. | 1) Find where revocation is checked (gateway, auth middleware, IdP, introspection, deny list lookup). 2) Check it runs outside the agent process. 3) Answer no if revocation sends a message or signal to the agent and relies on it to drop credentials, or the check runs inside agent code.\n  B04.3 Later calls are blocked even with cached tokens, self-contained tokens, and open sessions: token lifetime is shorter than the revocation limit, or revocation is pushed to each service (for example CAEP) or checked on every call. | 1) Find access-token lifetimes and how services validate tokens (signature only vs `introspect`, deny list, CAEP). 2) Compare lifetime with the stated revocation limit. 3) Answer no if self-contained JWTs outlive the limit and are checked by signature only, cached tokens or open sessions skip the check, or no limit is stated. Answer unknown if validation lives outside this repo.\n  B04.4 Revocation covers access tokens and open sessions, not only the refresh token. | 1) Find the revoke code or runbook. 2) List what it revokes: refresh token, access tokens, sessions, streams. 3) Answer no if it only revokes the refresh token or deletes stored credentials while issued access tokens and open sessions stay valid.\nB05 Destructive-action interception\n  B05.1 High-impact operations are listed in code or config and blocked by default, including deletes, destructive updates, force-pushes, infrastructure teardown, payment changes, outbound sends, and deleting audit records or changing their retention. | 1) Search code and config for a list of high-impact operations (deny/approval policy, OPA/Rego, Cedar, hook config). 2) Check it covers deletes, destructive updates, force-push, teardown, payments, outbound sends, audit deletion or retention changes. 3) Answer no if no list exists, any category is missing with a tool that can do it, or unlisted operations default to `allow`.\n  B05.2 The gate runs in the execution path when the call runs and checks the operation, the target, and the arguments, not only a tool name, command name, or blocklist of words. | 1) Find where tool calls execute (`tool_use`, `call_tool`, `execute`, `can_use_tool`, `PreToolUse`). 2) Find the gate that runs before the side effect and see what it inspects. 3) Answer no if it checks only the tool or command name, matches words (`'rm' in cmd`, `DROP`), runs after the call, lives only in the prompt, or sits in a `try`/`catch` that continues on error. 4) For each value that lets an action skip approval (an internal recipient, a small amount, a safe path), read the comparison itself: answer no if a domain, host, or path is matched by suffix, prefix, or substring without an anchor, so `endswith('example.com')` also passes `evil-example.com`.\n  B05.3 The same gate covers every path that can do the operation, including raw shell, HTTP or API clients, other tools, and requests to a more powerful tool or peer agent. | 1) Open the tool registry or config and write every tool the model can call into your note, by name, including MCP servers and sub-agents. 2) For each, say whether it can do a gated operation, including through a page it controls: a browser click or form fill in a logged-in session can buy, send, or delete. 3) Trace each such tool to the gate. 4) Answer no if any alias, raw shell or HTTP tool, browser action, second dispatch path, peer agent, or more powerful MCP tool can do the operation without passing the same gate.\n  B05.4 A blocked operation runs only after approval from a person or system with higher authority than the agent; a prompt, comment, or model instruction is never the enforcement. | 1) Find what happens when the gate blocks (approval request, `interrupt()`, `needs_approval`, webhook to reviewer). 2) Check who approves. 3) Answer no if the model, the agent itself, or a prompt instruction can approve, if approval is auto-granted (`bypassPermissions`, `acceptEdits`, auto-approve flag, `yes` default), or if a timeout proceeds.\n  B05.5 For an agent that reads untrusted input, reaches sensitive data or systems, and acts outside, every consequential action is gated, unless code shows untrusted content cannot pick the tool, destination, or arguments (for example plan-then-execute or capability tracking). | 1) Check whether the agent reads untrusted content (web, email, files, tool output), reaches sensitive data, and can act outside. 2) If all three, list consequential actions and check each is gated. 3) Answer no if any is ungated and untrusted content can choose its tool, destination, or arguments with no plan-then-execute or capability tracking in code. Answer yes if the three are not combined.\n<!-- Generated by skills/gen_references.py from CHECKLIST.md, CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md, ROLES.md, and heuristics. Do not edit by hand. -->\n\n# B — Build-time\n\nUse this file to rate each item from the code and configuration. The requirement is the checklist text. The heuristics say what to look for. Ratings: likely_pass, gap, needs_evidence, or na_candidate. Never rate pass.\n\n## B01 · G1 · C4 — Minimal tool surface\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Security.\n\n### Requirement\n\nList every built-in tool, MCP server, API, program, and file permission this agent type can use. Give a task-based reason for each. Enforce a role-based allowlist, and check that an unlisted tool can't be called. Don't base allow or approval decisions on hints a server gives about its own tools, such as \"read-only.\" MCP treats those hints as untrusted unless you trust the server.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nExport the tools, programs, and file permissions the running agent can see. Compare them with the approved list. Try an unlisted tool through the harness and through another path that could call it. Both must be denied, while an allowed tool still works. Change a server's tool hint so a write tool claims to be read-only. The allow or approval decision must not change.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** List comparison, allowlist, traces of allowed and denied calls.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **B01.1** Each agent role has one explicit allowlist of tool names, with no wildcard grants such as `*`, `mcp__*`, `Bash(*)`, or `bypassPermissions`.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where each agent is built and tools are passed (`allowed_tools`, `tools=[`, `allowedTools`, `.mcp.json`, `settings.json`). 2) Check each role has one fixed list of names. 3) Answer no if any list holds `*`, `mcp__*`, `Bash(*)`, a pattern or regex, `bypassPermissions`, or is built from `list_tools()`, or if a second agent role or entry point has no list.\n- **B01.2** Tools discovered from MCP servers are filtered against that allowlist before the model can see or call them.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find MCP discovery (`list_tools`, `session.list_tools`, `tools/list`, MCP client setup). 2) Trace the result to where it is handed to the model. 3) Confirm a filter keeps only names on the allowlist. 4) Answer no if any discovered tool reaches the model unfiltered, the filter is a denylist or prefix match (`startswith`, `mcp__server__`), or a refresh or reconnect path skips it.\n- **B01.3** The dispatcher rejects a call to any tool name not on the allowlist, instead of looking it up dynamically.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the dispatcher (search `tool_use`, `call_tool`, `dispatch`, `execute`, `getattr`, `registry[`). 2) Check it rejects names not on the allowlist with an error. 3) Answer no if it looks tools up with `getattr`, `globals()`, `eval`, or a registry holding more than the allowlist, falls back to calling the MCP server directly, or catches the unknown-tool error and continues.\n- **B01.4** Allow and approval decisions never read server-supplied hints or descriptions such as `readOnlyHint` or 'read-only'.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Search for `readOnlyHint`, `destructiveHint`, `annotations`, `description`, and approval or auto-approve code. 2) Check whether any allow, skip-approval, or permission branch reads these. 3) Answer no if any allow or approval decision depends on a server hint or tool description text, including a default that auto-approves tools marked read-only.\n- **B01.5** File access is limited to named directories, not the whole filesystem.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find file tools and mounts (`allowed_directories`, `cwd`, `open(`, `fs.`, `os.ReadFile`, container `volumes`, `mountPath`). 2) Check paths are resolved (`realpath`, `resolve`, `filepath.Clean`) then compared to named directories. 3) Answer no if access is unrestricted, `/` or home is mounted, the check is a string `startsWith`/prefix test without resolving `..` and symlinks, or a shell tool bypasses it.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- A tool call that skips the MCP gateway or reaches a tool server directly → **E06**\n- A token or credential with too much access → **B02**\n- Hidden instructions inside a tool's description or schema → **E04**\n- A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved → **E03**\n- Lookalike or duplicate tool names across servers → **E05**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- One explicit allowlist of tools per agent role, for example `allowed_tools=[...]` in the Claude Agent SDK, a fixed `tools=[...]` list on an OpenAI Agents SDK `Agent`, or a named list passed to a LangChain/LangGraph agent.\n- MCP tools filtered by name against that allowlist after discovery, for example a `tool_filter`, `allowedTools`, or `disallowedTools` setting, or code that drops any tool not on the list.\n- A written inventory (README, `.brace/` record, or config comments) that lists every tool, MCP server, API, program, and file path with a task-based reason for each.\n- The dispatcher rejects a call to an unknown tool name with an error, instead of looking it up dynamically.\n- File access limited to named directories (for example `allowed_directories`, `cwd` sandboxing, or a read-only mount), not the whole filesystem.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Tool list built from `server.list_tools()` or `session.list_tools()` with no allowlist filter, so any tool a server adds becomes callable.\n- Allow or auto-approve decisions keyed on server hints such as `readOnlyHint`, `destructiveHint`, or a tool description that says 'read-only'.\n- Wildcard tool grants, for example `allowed_tools=['*']`, `mcp__*`, `Bash(*)`, or `permission_mode='bypassPermissions'`.\n- A generic tool such as unrestricted shell, `eval`, `exec`, or HTTP fetch to any URL, with no stated task reason.\n- `.mcp.json` / `mcp.json` / `claude_desktop_config.json` lists servers that no task in the inventory needs.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Agent construction code (where tools are registered or passed to the model)\n- Tool dispatch or router code in the harness\n- *.mcp.json, mcp.json, claude_desktop_config.json, settings.json permission blocks\n- Claude Agent SDK options (`allowed_tools`, `disallowed_tools`, `permission_mode`, `can_use_tool`)\n- Container mounts and volume definitions for file permissions\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Code shows the intended list, not what the running agent can see. Recipe B01: export the live tool list, compare with the approved list, try an unlisted tool through the harness and another path, and flip a server hint to confirm decisions don't change.\n\n**N/A when:** never