{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019iRKtx8A9LFFuSsvS3JMv5","type":"tool_result","content":"144-### Requirement\n146-Issue short-lived credentials from a trusted issuer or managed identity service. For API calls, use expiring access tokens with a specific destination audience, identifying the agent and any explicitly delegated user or system authority, rather than static API keys or personal access tokens. A secrets vault stores backing secrets; it does not itself make a static key into an expiring access token. Keep long-lived secrets out of prompts, images, and checked-in config. Where supported, bind tokens to their holder (mTLS, DPoP, or workload proof tokens). Don't use fixed API keys when short-lived or federated credentials are available. If only static keys are supported, isolate them at the enforcement layer, limit their permissions, and record the lifetime and revocation gap. Show expiry and rotation, including allowed overlap, and explain the chosen lifetime.\n148-### Verification recipe\n150-Obtain a short-lived API access token from the destination's trusted issuer using the configured machine or delegated-user flow. Call before and after expiry, rotate, and retry the old credential. Expired tokens fail; replaced credentials fail after documented overlap. Replay a holder-bound token from a different machine/key where supported. Distinguish token issuance from backing-secret storage. For static-key-only services, demonstrate enforcement-layer isolation and record the expiry/revocation gap. Scan prompts, image layers, and config for long-lived secrets.\n207-### Requirement\n209-Show that an operator can cut off this agent's access without cutting off the user who launched it. The agent must not need to cooperate. Set and measure the longest time before the cutoff takes effect. Count cached tokens, self-contained tokens, and open sessions. A trusted enforcement point must block later calls within a time limit that fits the potential harm. Revoking only the refresh token is not enough. If tokens live longer than that limit, push the revocation to each service (for example, with OpenID CAEP session-revoked events) or make the tokens shorter-lived.\n211-### Verification recipe\n213-Open a session and revoke the agent identity's access. Then try again, within the set revocation time, through the existing session and a new one. Check self-contained and cached tokens, refresh-token issuing, and open sessions. The agent's requests must fail within the time limit at a trusted enforcement point. The user's unrelated requests must still work. If you push revocations, record when each service received and applied the event.\n268-### Requirement\n270-List the high-impact operations. These include deletes, destructive database updates, force-pushes, tearing down infrastructure, payment changes, and sending anything outside. Block them by default in the execution path. Allow one only when a reviewer or system with higher authority approves that specific action. When the action runs, authorize its operation, target, and arguments, and block it if they aren't allowed. A list of blocked words or command names is not enough. Test other tools and raw API or shell paths that could do the same thing. Treat deleting audit records, or changing how long they are kept, as high-impact too. That includes asking a more powerful tool or peer to do it. Some agents can do all three of these in one context: read untrusted input, reach sensitive data or systems, and change things or talk to the outside. For those agents, treat every consequential action as high-impact. The exception is a design that stops untrusted content from picking the tool, destination, or arguments. Examples are plan-then-execute, a two-model pattern, or capability tracking such as CaMeL. Record which of the three each agent type has.\n272-### Verification recipe\n274-Aim each forbidden operation at throwaway resources through every tool,